      "writtenOn": "2025-12-11T23:19:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm... so, in legacy mode we do care to put the iommu domain, but in scalable mode we don\u0027t even check if there are any domains still associated with any PASID entries behind this context entry?\n\n...Question: when this hypercall is called in scalable mode, are there still any domains associated with PASID entries behind this context entry? IOW, does the host call pkvm_hc_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() for all those PASID entries before calling pkvm_hc_iommu_clear_ce() for this context entry, or not? (I suppose it doesn\u0027t.)",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "379fbab8_dbc3bfb0",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T00:34:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If I understand correctly, the iommu subsystem considers it okay to have the context cleared before pasid entries are cleared. During a hot-unplug scenario, i think the context gets cleared first during device cleanup, but PASIDs attached to processes or VMS will be cleared later only.\n\nThis was my understanding and from the code I could not see a device removal code path or iommu group removal code path(both does SM context clear) directly trying to remove pasid entries. Pasid entry gets cleared when a device gets detached from domain or a process or VM unbinds a device.",
      "parentUuid": "7753e1c9_a78303dd",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a2619124_01f9f478",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T01:03:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So, the host clears the context entry without bothering to clear its child PASID entries, and that in itself is fine (no need to clear them, just need to free them), but for us (for pKVM) it means that the host will never issue the clear_pasid_entry hypercall for those PASID entries, thus we will never decrease the refcount of their corresponding IOMMU domains.\n\nSo we have a resource leak, and a potential functionality issue: the host will try to free an IOMMU domain later, and will fail, since pKVM will return an error, since the domain\u0027s refcount is \u003e1?\n\nSo it seems like we should put the iommu domains in pkvm_pasid_free_table()?",
      "parentUuid": "7753e1c9_a78303dd",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2ca17bf3_0c732d93",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T01:14:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the host will issue clear_pasid_entry when the process or device is unbound and the domain will be freed only after all the attached pasid entries are unbound. From my understanding, domain free is not dependent on SM context entry clear, but the attached PASIDs \n\nI am unable to test this with pKVM as we do not support device assignment. In the normal case we attach the device with IOMMU_NO_PASID and we have verified that the domain gets freed as expected when we detach the domain(dma to identity).",
      "parentUuid": "a2619124_01f9f478",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c29f1e1c_a888335c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T01:17:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Furthermore, putting the iommu domain is not the only thing we need to do when a PASID entry goes away: we also need to decrease `pt_cnt` if needed, and unassign the cache tags if needed? (i.e. the same things that  pkvm_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() does)\n\nSo seems like we should do all the same in pkvm_pasid_free_table() as well, for each present PASID entry, in each present PASID table? (Perhaps introduce a handy helper to use in both pkvm_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() and pkvm_pasid_free_table()?)\n\nSo, the scope of this bug extends beyond this patch:\n\n* The 1st buggy patch is aosp/3818903 which decreases pt_cnt in pkvm_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() but not in pkvm_pasid_free_table().\n* The 2nd buggy patch is this patch, which puts the iommu domain in  pkvm_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() but not in pkvm_pasid_free_table().\n* The 3rd buggy patch is aosp/3788755 which unassigns the cache tags in pkvm_iommu_clear_pasid_entry() but not in pkvm_pasid_free_table().\n\nCorrect?",
      "parentUuid": "a2619124_01f9f478",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d75b3bc7_06295c09",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T01:31:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding is - If the host driver works as designed, then we would get clear_pasid_entry from host for all attached PASIDs before a domain free. A compromised host can perform operations out of order, but I guess securitywise, it should not break any guarantees.\n\nIn our current case, we get only IOMMU_NO_PASID attach and the clear_pasid_entry is guaranteed to be called before domain free. For simplicity, shall we defer the verification of PASID clear scenarois to later when we support device assignment? Probably we can put a check to see we don\u0027t get any pasid attach other than IOMMU_NO_PASID? WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "c29f1e1c_a888335c",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6dfc8724_700a1e80",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T02:16:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You said yourself: \"If I understand correctly, the iommu subsystem considers it okay to have the context cleared before pasid entries are cleared. During a hot-unplug scenario, i think the context gets cleared first during device cleanup, but PASIDs attached to processes or VMS will be cleared later only.\"\n\nwhich actually means that those PASID entries will not be cleared at all (because there is no need to clear them, since there is no context entry that would point to them): in intel_iommu_release_device(), the host first clears the context entry in intel_pasid_teardown_sm_context(), and right after that it frees the PASID tables in intel_pasid_free_table(). After that, the host cannot clear the PASID entries in those tables even if it needed to, since those pages have been freed and may be used for something else now. Right?\n\n\u003e I am unable to test this with pKVM as we do not support device assignment. \n\nVFIO still supports it, as usual, right? We (pKVM) don\u0027t support assigning devices to pVMs, but for npVMs it might just work (although I haven\u0027t tried since a while). And even if it doesn\u0027t work (i.e. if the device itself is not operational in the VM), that is not our concern here, our concern is that attaching/detaching the device to a domain by the IOMMU subsystem in the host still works correctly.\n\nSo for a test we might simply try assigning a PCI device to a VM via vfio-pci in the usual way (i.e. as if there was no pKVM), and see what happens, and how the host behaves (which hypercalls it issues to pKVM in which order, etc)...\n\n\u003e Probably we can put a check to see we don\u0027t get any pasid attach other than IOMMU_NO_PASID?\n\nThat is orthogonal? Our hardware (PTL) doesn\u0027t even support any other PASIDs than IOMMU_NO_PASID, yes, but we are not talking about other PASIDs, we are talking about correct teardown of a PASID entry when clearing a context entry in scalable mode, no matter for which PASID.",
      "parentUuid": "d75b3bc7_06295c09",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87247e99_4d9b8570",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 84
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-12T17:37:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, as we figured out offline, at least in basic scenario of detaching a device from a domain, the host actually first clears the PASID entry (in a non-intuitive path: iommu_deinit_device() -\u003e blocking_domain_attach_dev() -\u003e device_block_translation() -\u003e intel_pasid_tear_down_entry()) *before* clearing the context entry (in iommu_deinit_device() -\u003e intel_iommu_release_device() -\u003e intel_pasid_teardown_sm_context()).\n\nSo we can merge phase2 patches is, and then maybe, for example, in phase3 integrate Aashish\u0027s patch https://github.com/vineethrp/pKVM-IA/commit/bb1337730366e83bfa93a4878b90fdd4d1fef182 for releasing resources associated with the orphaned PASID entries in pkvm_pasid_free_table() if there are any, i.e. just in case the host hasn\u0027t cleared them before clearing the context entry. (Since even if the host driver doesn\u0027t currently behave this way in any scenarios, it still seems to be a legitimate behavior.)",
      "parentUuid": "6dfc8724_700a1e80",
      "revId": "78c41d21f5e4117220a790e69f08e5af1a51c0c3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
